1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,461
This video introduces nondeterministic finite automata, or N F As.

2
00:00:05,911 --> 00:00:22,103
We define how an N F A accepts a string, using runs. Then we define the language of a regex and of an automaton, and regular languages. At the end we solve the questions-pre section on flavours of nondeterminism.

3
00:00:22,553 --> 00:00:28,399
In the last video, a D F A had exactly one arrow for each state and each letter.

4
00:00:28,849 --> 00:00:34,118
So the next state is a function of the state and the letter. We call that function delta.

5
00:00:34,568 --> 00:00:42,632
An N F A drops this rule. From one state, one letter may lead to several states. Here, a leads to two states.

6
00:00:43,082 --> 00:00:55,839
The machine may also move on no letter at all. That is an epsilon edge. And a letter may have no arrow. To show the change, the slides write a capital Delta, not a small delta.

7
00:00:56,289 --> 00:01:03,927
Here is the N F A from the slides. Start state q0. Accepting state q2, with the double border.

8
00:01:04,377 --> 00:01:12,633
From q0, the letter a has two arrows. One goes to q3. One goes to q1. The machine has a choice.

9
00:01:13,083 --> 00:01:21,787
Which arrow does it take? On the slides, you are the oracle. You know the whole input and the machine. You tell it which way to go.

10
00:01:22,237 --> 00:01:32,263
Does it accept a? Read a, and go to q1 or to q3. The input ends there. Neither state accepts. So no choice helps. It rejects a.

11
00:01:32,713 --> 00:01:41,225
Does it accept a a? Choose q1 on the first a. Then the second a goes to q2, which accepts. So it accepts a a.

12
00:01:41,675 --> 00:01:55,371
The rule is this. The N F A accepts w if some choice of moves accepts it. The choice is not random, and it is not probabilistic. If an oracle could guide it to accept, it accepts.

13
00:01:55,821 --> 00:02:01,560
There is a second way to say the same thing. Try one way. Take the first a to q3.

14
00:02:02,010 --> 00:02:06,319
The second a has no arrow out of q3. This is a dead end.

15
00:02:06,769 --> 00:02:11,783
On a dead end, backtrack. Go back to the last choice, and try a different arrow.

16
00:02:12,233 --> 00:02:21,555
Now take the first a to q1. The second a goes to q2. The input ends in an accepting state. So the machine accepts a a.

17
00:02:22,005 --> 00:02:33,184
Both views give the same answer. The language of this machine is the set with one string, a a. Nobody has to build the oracle. Next week we compile it away.

18
00:02:33,634 --> 00:02:43,362
N F As allow two conveniences. The first is the epsilon transition. Here are two small machines. Each accepts the string a.

19
00:02:43,812 --> 00:02:51,663
An epsilon transition moves from one state to another without reading a letter. Epsilon is the empty string.

20
00:02:52,113 --> 00:03:03,889
Epsilon edges are the glue. They say: when this machine is done, start that one. We join the two machines with one epsilon edge. We do not have to merge any states.

21
00:03:04,339 --> 00:03:16,520
The result reads a, moves for free from q1 to q2, then reads a. It accepts a a. Next video, the four templates use this glue for every regex construct.

22
00:03:16,970 --> 00:03:28,874
The second convenience is missing transitions. A D F A table is complete: every state, every letter. Here is a D F A for the string a a, with the alphabet just a.

23
00:03:29,324 --> 00:03:40,012
After a a, one more a must still go somewhere. So the D F A needs an error state, X, with a self loop. Every longer string ends in X and is rejected.

24
00:03:40,462 --> 00:03:54,073
In an N F A drawing, we omit those arrows. A missing arrow means reject from here. The two machines accept the same strings. A python check compared them on all strings up to length 8.

25
00:03:54,523 --> 00:04:06,320
N F As are convenient to draw and to build. They are harder to execute. That is fine. The N F A is the intermediate language of our compiler, not the target.

26
00:04:06,770 --> 00:04:21,917
Formally, an N F A has the same tuple as a D F A. Only the function delta is replaced by a relation, capital Delta. Capital Delta is a subset of Q, times sigma plus epsilon, times Q.

27
00:04:22,367 --> 00:04:34,420
So capital Delta is a set of triples. A triple q, x, q prime means an arrow from q to q prime with label x. The label is a letter or epsilon.

28
00:04:34,870 --> 00:04:40,395
Write capital Delta for this machine. The first arrow: q0, a, q1.

29
00:04:40,845 --> 00:04:43,853
The second arrow: q0, a, q3.

30
00:04:44,303 --> 00:04:51,066
The third arrow: q1, a, q2. That is all the arrows. Capital Delta has three triples.

31
00:04:51,516 --> 00:04:55,079
Now count the triples that leave each state on the letter a.

32
00:04:55,529 --> 00:05:00,947
From q0 on a, two triples leave. That is the choice we saw before.

33
00:05:01,397 --> 00:05:10,400
From q1 on a, one triple leaves. From q2 and q3 on a, none leave. A missing arrow is just a missing triple.

34
00:05:10,850 --> 00:05:24,013
A D F A is the special case of an N F A. It has exactly one triple for each state and letter, and none on epsilon. Then capital Delta is the graph of the function small delta.

35
00:05:24,463 --> 00:05:33,999
To make the oracle precise, the slides define a run. A run of M on w is a path from the initial state that spells w.

36
00:05:34,449 --> 00:05:50,854
A run has three conditions. It starts at the initial state. Every step is an arrow in capital Delta, with a letter or epsilon. And the labels, written in order, spell the input. Epsilon is the empty string, so it vanishes.

37
00:05:51,304 --> 00:05:55,101
Find a run of this machine on a a. Start at q0.

38
00:05:55,551 --> 00:05:59,690
Read the first a. Take the arrow from q0 to q1.

39
00:06:00,140 --> 00:06:05,089
Now take the epsilon edge from q1 to q2. It reads no letter.

40
00:06:05,539 --> 00:06:19,897
Read the second a, from q2 to q3. The labels are a, epsilon, a. That spells a a. The run has three steps for two letters. So m, the number of steps, can be greater than the length of w.

41
00:06:20,347 --> 00:06:27,429
A run is accepting if it ends in an accepting state. That is, the last state r m is in F.

42
00:06:27,879 --> 00:06:34,023
M accepts w if some run on w is accepting. One accepting run is enough.

43
00:06:34,473 --> 00:06:41,343
M rejects w if no run is accepting. This includes the case where there is no run at all.

44
00:06:41,793 --> 00:06:49,857
The language of M is the set of all strings w over sigma for which some run of M on w is accepting.

45
00:06:50,307 --> 00:06:56,707
Now list the runs of the slide machine on a, a a, and a a a. Pause the video and try it.

46
00:06:57,157 --> 00:07:06,522
On a, there are two runs. q0 to q1, and q0 to q3. Neither ends in F. So the machine rejects a.

47
00:07:06,972 --> 00:07:11,964
A run must spell the whole input. A run that stops early does not count.

48
00:07:12,414 --> 00:07:25,278
On a a, there is one run: q0, q1, q2. It ends in F, so the machine accepts a a. The path through q3 is stuck after one a. It is not a run on a a.

49
00:07:25,728 --> 00:07:33,536
On a a a, there is no run at all. After q2, no arrow reads the third a. So the machine rejects a a a.

50
00:07:33,986 --> 00:07:48,343
A D F A has exactly one run on every string, the one delta star computes. So for a D F A, some run means the run. The definition agrees with the D F A language from the last video.

51
00:07:48,793 --> 00:07:55,599
Recall that a language over an alphabet sigma is a set of strings. It is a subset of sigma star.

52
00:07:56,049 --> 00:08:09,979
The language of a regular expression r is the set of strings that match r. Matching means the whole string, not just a substring. For r equal to a b star, a, a b and a b b match.

53
00:08:10,429 --> 00:08:29,032
The language of an automaton A is the set of strings that A accepts. A must consume the whole input and end in an accepting state. For an N F A, along some run. The string a b a b contains a b, but the whole string does not match. Both say no.

54
00:08:29,482 --> 00:08:46,741
L of r equals L of A means they describe exactly the same strings. For every w, w matches r if and only if A accepts w. A python check compared this machine and this regex on all strings up to length 8.

55
00:08:47,191 --> 00:08:59,244
Now the main definition. A language L is regular if some D F A accepts it. In program terms, you can write its recognizer with finite memory, loops and ifs.

56
00:08:59,694 --> 00:09:04,217
Two theorems connect regexes and automata through their languages.

57
00:09:04,667 --> 00:09:18,853
Theorem 1. For every regular expression r, there is a D F A A with the same language. We establish this by compiling a regex into a D F A. That compiler is the rest of this unit.

58
00:09:19,303 --> 00:09:29,671
Theorem 2 goes the other way. For every finite automaton, there is a regex with the same language. The course does not establish this direction.

59
00:09:30,121 --> 00:09:36,116
Together, the two theorems say that regexes describe exactly the regular languages.

60
00:09:36,566 --> 00:09:47,126
Does the choice give an N F A more power than a D F A? The course notes answer this. A finite state machine has no storage beyond its current state.

61
00:09:47,576 --> 00:09:59,693
More powerful machines add storage. A pushdown automaton adds a stack. A linear bounded automaton adds a finite tape. A Turing machine adds an infinite tape.

62
00:10:00,143 --> 00:10:16,058
For finite automata, nondeterminism adds nothing. D F As and N F As are equivalent in power and speed. Both run in linear time. But an N F A might have exponentially fewer states than a D F A.

63
00:10:16,508 --> 00:10:30,759
For pushdown automata, nondeterminism does add power. For Turing machines, it adds speed but not power. This course compiles every N F A into a D F A, so the oracle is never built.

64
00:10:31,209 --> 00:10:40,659
Not every language is regular. The first example is a to the n, b to the n. That is n copies of a, then n copies of b.

65
00:10:41,109 --> 00:10:48,555
a a b b is in the language, with n equal to 2. The empty string is in it too, with n equal to 0.

66
00:10:49,005 --> 00:10:57,005
a a b is not in the language, because the counts are unequal. a b a b is not in it, because the order is wrong.

67
00:10:57,455 --> 00:11:14,905
To check these strings, a machine must remember how many a's it read. That count has no bound. Checking it needs more memory than a D F A has. So this language is not regular. A formal proof uses the pumping lemma. The slides only point to it.

68
00:11:15,355 --> 00:11:26,235
The second example is addition in unary. 1 to the n means n copies of 1. Plus and equals are literal symbols. Zero is an empty block.

69
00:11:26,685 --> 00:11:34,301
1 1 plus 1 1 1 equals 1 1 1 1 1 is in the language. It says 2 plus 3 equals 5.

70
00:11:34,751 --> 00:11:42,218
1 1 plus 1 1 1 equals 1 1 1 1 is not in the language, because 2 plus 3 is not 4.

71
00:11:42,668 --> 00:11:51,457
The machine must remember n plus m until it reads the last block. That count has no bound. So neither language is regular.

72
00:11:51,907 --> 00:12:11,193
Now the questions-pre section on flavours of nondeterminism. The N F A starts at state 1 and accepts at state 3. It gets stuck at state 2. The input is the string x. Which state does it go to? Answer for angelic, demonic, and arbitrary nondeterminism.

73
00:12:11,643 --> 00:12:13,285
Pause the video and try it.

74
00:12:13,735 --> 00:12:22,887
On x, there are two runs. One ends in state 2. One ends in state 3. The flavour says which choice the machine makes.

75
00:12:23,337 --> 00:12:30,441
Angelic nondeterminism picks the choice that leads to acceptance. The posted answer is state 3.

76
00:12:30,891 --> 00:12:38,955
Demonic nondeterminism picks the choice that leads to rejection. The posted answer is state 2, where it is stuck.

77
00:12:39,405 --> 00:12:47,192
Arbitrary nondeterminism picks any choice. You cannot know which. The posted answer is either 2 or 3.

78
00:12:47,642 --> 00:13:00,101
Our acceptance rule, some run is accepting, is the angelic flavour. The oracle always guides the machine to accept, when that is possible. So this N F A accepts x.

79
00:13:00,551 --> 00:13:11,025
The last question in this section. Can a regular expression for recognizing H T M L be written? Why or why not? Pause the video and try it.

80
00:13:11,475 --> 00:13:19,966
Here is the example fragment from the question. Each open tag has a matching close tag, and tags nest inside each other.

81
00:13:20,416 --> 00:13:29,675
The depth goes up at each open tag and down at each close tag. To check the close tags, a recognizer must remember this depth.

82
00:13:30,125 --> 00:13:38,765
Tags can nest to any depth. Open tags must match close tags. That is the same problem as a to the n, b to the n.

83
00:13:39,215 --> 00:13:48,303
So the answer is no. H T M L is not a regular language, because it has nested tags. A regex cannot recognize it.

84
00:13:48,753 --> 00:14:02,001
To summarize. An N F A replaces the function delta with a relation, capital Delta, a set of triples. A letter may lead to several states, or none, and a move may read epsilon.

85
00:14:02,451 --> 00:14:11,347
A run is a path from the start state whose labels spell the input. Epsilon vanishes, so a run can have more steps than letters.

86
00:14:11,797 --> 00:14:21,375
M accepts w if some run ends in an accepting state. No run at all means reject. This is angelic nondeterminism.

87
00:14:21,825 --> 00:14:30,636
The language of a regex or an automaton contains whole strings only. A language is regular if some D F A accepts it.

88
00:14:31,086 --> 00:14:44,889
a to the n, b to the n, unary addition, and H T M L need unbounded counts, so they are not regular. Next video: compile a regex into an N F A with four templates.
